# MAN(1) [April 19, 1988]

## NAME

man - print out the manual

## SYNOPSIS

| **man** [ **-** ] [ **-a** ] [ **-w** ] [ **-M** *path* ] [ *section* ] *title* ...

## DESCRIPTION

*Man* provides on-line access to this manual. If a *section* specifier is given, *man* looks in
that section for the given *title*(s); if it is omitted, *man* searches every section, giving
preference to commands over library subroutines, and displays the first page it finds.

A section is a digit, optionally followed by the subsection letter that appears on the page's
own title: `1`, `1m`, `2`, `3`, `3s`, `3m`, `3x`, `5`, `8`. A bare digit names the whole family,
so `man 3 stdio` finds *stdio*(3S) and `man 1 fsck` finds *fsck*(1M); a digit and a letter must
match exactly. The search order is 1, 1M, 8, 6, 2, 3, 3S, 3M, 3X, 4, 5, 7 -- commands first --
and it is what decides which page a bare *title* gets when the same name appears in two
sections. `man mount` is *mount*(1M), the command; `man 2 mount` is the system call.

The options are:

**-**
: Copy the page to the standard output as it stands, with no pager. This is the source of the
  page, in the dialect described below.

**-a**
: Display all the pages that match, rather than the first.

**-w**
: Print where the page is, one path to a line, and display nothing.

**-M** *path*
: Search *path* instead of the default `/usr/man`. *Path* is a colon-separated list of
  directories, each expected to hold the standard `man?` subdirectories; the whole of one
  directory is searched before the next is looked at. The environment variable *MANPATH* says
  the same thing.

If the standard output is a terminal and the **-** flag is not given, *man* pipes the page
through more(1), or through the pager named by the environment variable *PAGER*.

*Note:* **this program does not format.** There is no nroff(1) on this machine, and none is
coming; the pages here are written in the dialect `doc/Manual_Page_Format.md` describes and are
meant to be read as they stand. So *man* runs cat(1) where another system runs a formatter, and
the difference between `man ls` and `man - ls` is the pager and nothing else. When a renderer
exists it takes cat(1)'s place, and nothing about the sections, the paths or the options above
changes.

*Note:* the sections `local`, `new` and `old`, the *MACHINE* variable and the machine-specific
subdirectories it selected, the preformatted `cat?` directories and the `3f` FORTRAN section are
all gone. This manual has one tree, one copy of each page, and no FORTRAN. The undocumented
**-f** and **-k**, which invoked whatis(1) and apropos(1), are gone with them: neither program
is on this system, and there is no `whatis` database for one to read.

## FILES

| `/usr/man` the manual
| `/usr/man/man?/*` the pages themselves
| `/bin/cat` what a page is shown with
| `/bin/more` what it is paged with

## SEE ALSO

cat(1), more(1)

## BUGS

The command *man* builds is handed to sh(1), so a *title* that names a real page and also
contains a shell metacharacter is re-interpreted by the shell. Every *man* of this lineage has
this hole.

A section is recognised by its leading digit, so a page whose name begins with a digit could not
be asked for by name alone. There is no such page.

A subsection letter that no version of this program has seen -- `2v`, say -- is staged onto the
disk correctly and cannot be found here until it is added to the table in the source.

The exit status of the pager, and of the program that shows the page, is discarded.
